
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the identifier survives closing the browser. That decides the storage mechanism, and storage
  mechanism is the part that's painful to change later because every consumer reads through it.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and navigation within the tab, gone when the tab closes. Per-tab, so two tabs are
    two separate sessions. The conservative default: on a shared or public machine, the identity doesn't outlive the
    visit.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restarts, shared across tabs, persists until explicitly cleared. This is what
    "persists" usually means colloquially, and it's what you'd want if returning users should be recognized. Cost: on a
    shared machine the last user's identity is still sitting there at the next visit, so you need an explicit logout
    path that clears it — otherwise you've built an identity that can't be shed.
  - [1mIn-memory module state[0m — simplest and leaks nothing, but it's erased on every reload. I think this fails your
    "persists" requirement; I list it only so the rejection is on the record.

  One thing I want to flag rather than decide silently: whatever we store, I'd store [1monly the userId[0m, never the password
  and not an auth token. A userId in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is low-stakes — it's an identifier, readable by any script on the
  page but not a credential. The moment a token joins it, the same storage choice becomes a real security decision with
  a much higher cost of being wrong. If you expect a session token later, say so now and I'll design the boundary for it
  instead of retrofitting.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with an explicit clear-on-logout function built in the same change. It matches
  "persists," and the shared-machine hazard is real but fully handled by making logout part of the design rather than a
  later addition.
